1. Introduction
Over just a few years, Anemoi has evolved from concept to a key component of the
European Meteorological software infrastructure, co-developed by ECMWF and many
national meteorological services across Europe and open to use and contributions by
the wider international community.
Anemoi provides a software ecosystem covering the key steps needed to develop datadriven forecasting systems, including dataset creation, model training, inference,
testing and workflow integration. Anemoi has enabled multiple organisations to
operationalise data-driven forecasting and is currently used by over 15 of ECMWF’s
Member and Cooperating States to build, test and mature data-driven weather
forecasting modelling systems, as well as a range of other earth-system applications. In
the ECMWF Machine Learning Project (MLP, formerly known as the Machine Learning
Pilot Project, MLPP), Anemoi serves as key shared software infrastructure for exploring
and operationalising the science and technology of AI. Anemoi and the MLP also form a
practical backbone of the wider EUMETNET E-AI activity, enabling European national
meteorological services (NMS) to move jointly from ML experimentation towards
operational capability.
This rapid evolution and wider use make governance, robustness and long-term
sustainability increasingly important. Key here is maintaining the balance between
rapid adoption, to enable Anemoi to stay relevant in a fast-moving world, and
operational resilience, to ensure the reliability of the operational data-driven systems
that will provide Europe with trustworthy weather information. Sustainability in this
context includes not only governance, but also continued organisational commitments,
maintainable architecture, robust release and compatibility practices, documentation
and a sufficiently broad contributor community.
This paper reports on the current governance structures in place and the next steps for
Anemoi governance and oversight. It also provides an update on the major
developments in Anemoi over the past year, and an outlook for the coming period.
Finally, it highlights key results from the first Anemoi user and developer survey,
before providing an outlook for the coming years.
2. Governance and sustainability
Governance is important to Anemoi’s success. It needs to provide sufficient oversight to
support coordination, quality and trust, while keeping processes light enough for
Anemoi to remain agile and easy to contribute to.
The Anemoi Technical Subgroup (ATS), established in 2024, continues to meet weekly.
The group, attended by up to two representatives from each contributing organisation,
steers the technical evolution of Anemoi. Organisations currently attending ATS are
those actively supporting Anemoi through activities such as development, review,
testing, operational implementation or other sustained technical contributions;
namely: AEMET, DWD, DMI, FMI, MET Norway, GeoSphere, KNMI, Meteo France,
MeteoSwiss, RMI, UK Met Office, and ECMWF.
ECMWF/TAC/58(26)11 5 of 12
Governance arrangements for Anemoi are still evolving, and the respective decisionmaking, advisory and implementation responsibilities will need to be clarified as
Anemoi matures. Participation in future governance will need to reflect alignment with
Anemoi’s public-service purpose and willingness to assume longer-term stewardship
responsibilities.
ATS assesses candidate contributions to decide if new features, breaking changes,
deprecation and other code adaptations should proceed. This assessment is done at a
high level, running in tandem with peer code review which focusses on the technical
implementation. Minutes from the meetings are made available to attendees of ATS and
the MLP. ATS holds roadmap meetings approximately every six months, where
organisations, via their ATS representatives, propose and discuss large-scale code
evolution, ensuring alignment with operational model developments. The resulting
roadmap is made available to those co-developing Anemoi for awareness and
alignment.
A notable topic for ATS in recent years has been to provide an initial definition of the
scope of Anemoi. Anemoi was initially developed for data-driven weather forecasting,
but its scope has broadened as the underlying science and technology have advanced
rapidly. It is now being applied to other Earth-system modelling applications, including
Earth-system components, a climate emulator in DestinE, nowcasting and downscaling.
This makes it difficult to define its scope too narrowly at this stage. The following is the
current first scope statement for Anemoi, agreed within ATS and anticipated to evolve
and sharpen as the topic further matures. It is reproduced here as agreed by ATS; its
longer-term formulation, including whether it should be expressed less in terms of
specific model architectures, is an issue for future review.
What is Anemoi for, and what is it not?
Anemoi started out as a codebase for building data-driven weather forecasting
models. Since then, it has evolved to include additional tasks, such as
downscaling (super-resolution), time interpolation (temporal downscaling), and
autoencoding. What these applications share is the need to build models in which
information must be gathered, exchanged, and processed across the spatial
dimension. For these use cases, scalable and parallelisable graph and
transformer tooling is particularly valuable. Anemoi is not intended to address
all Earth-system ML problems. Problems that focus on strictly local networks or
on processing long temporal signals (e.g. LSTMs) share little in common with
Anemoi’s current focus, and the framework is therefore unlikely to provide
significant benefits for those use cases.
Who is Anemoi for?
Anemoi’s primary developers are based in operational meteorological centres,
where it is used to create operational data-driven models. Many of these
developers are also users of the framework, building and deploying models in
practice. This user group places a strong emphasis on structure and
reproducibility, in particular the ability to recreate the same models as Anemoi
evolves and expands.
6 of 12 ECMWF/TAC/58(26)11
Anemoi also aims to serve the wider research community by enabling users to
test new methods for training data-driven forecasting systems. This user group
places greater emphasis on flexibility, allowing ideas to be easily adapted and
tested.
Refining and enforcing the scope of Anemoi will be critical for Anemoi’s sustainability,
particularly in an era where LLM assisted coding lowers barriers allowing the
community to develop AI modelling solutions without these necessarily needing to be
implemented into Anemoi.
Feedback from across Europe, via numerous fora, highlighted a limitation with the
current Anemoi technical governance structure. Whilst broadly ATS was viewed as
steering Anemoi’s evolution effectively, a gap was identified in higher-level strategic
planning and cross-organisation prioritisation and resource management. To this end,
in the Autumn a first Anemoi review meeting will be organised. Attended by both
technical ATS members and senior representatives of contributing organisations, the
goal will be to take a high-level view on the progress and plans over the past and next
six months. It will also serve to identify strategic priorities, risks, and coordinate efforts
across the partners, thereby adding an additional layer of lightweight governance that
may further evolve based on needs being identified. The first meetings will also help
clarify the respective roles of ATS, of the review meeting and of contributing
organisations. This will include considering how priorities are set, how operational and
scientific needs are balanced, and which decisions and recommendations should be
documented and communicated more widely.
Ahead of the autumn review meeting, initial work has begun to assess how
contributions to Anemoi are currently distributed across organisations. The analysis
covers code contributions, including new features and bug fixes, as well as the
identification of technical issues and code reviews. Further work should broaden this
assessment to other aspects that support sustainability, including testing,
documentation, user support, operational adoption, datasets, computing resources and
funded development. Tracking these figures over time will help inform discussions on
Anemoi’s sustainability and the balance of partner contributions.
3. Highlights of Anemoi activities from the last year
This section summarises selected highlights from a very active year for Anemoi. Over
the past six months alone, 500 code contributions were merged into Anemoi, after
receiving over 2000 reviews from more than 15 organisations.
3.1. Computational performance enhancements
Extracting maximum computational performance, both in training and inference, is a
key aspect for Anemoi. With the ever-increasing price of GPUs, making the most value
of existing hardware is of utmost importance. Work undertaken in the past year has
improved the efficiency of Anemoi’s data loading, training and inference capabilities
which directly benefits all organisations using Anemoi.
ECMWF/TAC/58(26)11 7 of 12
This work was part of a broader shift from isolated model-level acceleration towards
end-to-end optimisation, covering training, inference, data movement, GPU kernels and
profiling. At high resolution, particular attention has been paid to removing memory
and I/O bottlenecks that limit scalability: keeping batches sharded through the full
training pipeline, improving mapper memory behaviour through edge sharding and
chunking and enabling parallel output during inference. In parallel, selected
performance-critical components have been moved from higher-level PyTorch/PyG
implementations towards custom Triton kernels, improving runtime efficiency. These
changes in concert have produced substantial practical gains, including more than a
fivefold reduction in training costs for a 9km model (see Figure 1). Such optimisations
directly benefit not only global model developments but are also directly applicable to
kilometre-scale regional models, particularly important for Member States.
Opportunities for efficiency gains are by no means exhausted and work will continue
over the next period with partners to identify shared computational inefficiencies and
work to rectify them.
