3.2. Testing
Testing is fundamental to ensuring Anemoi is robust and reliable for operational use.
As Anemoi matures, the testing infrastructure has evolved significantly in the last year.
The most important evolution is the explicit expansion of the test pyramid, Figure 2. A
test pyramid refers to a layered testing approach, with many small, fast tests which are
run frequently, and fewer larger, slower tests run less often. Anemoi's current pyramid
distinguishes unit tests, integration tests, and system-level tests.
Unit tests cover small units of code, ensuring the interfaces and behaviour of kernels do
not change without clear purpose.
Integration tests now cover three aspects: workflow tests; benchmark tests; and
accuracy tests.
Workflow tests maintain functionality in key use-cases for Anemoi, e.g. ensuring that
the interface to train a model has not changed. These tests can be fairly cheap, taking
order minutes, and therefore cover a broad spectrum of different use-cases. As with
system-level tests, this creates opportunities for organisation-specific tests. For 
8 of 12 ECMWF/TAC/58(26)11
example, one of the workflow tests is an example of training AICON, contributed by
DWD.
Benchmark tests provide assurances that code changes do not degrade computation
performance. These capture throughput of training, data-loading and memory used by
training. These tests cover global, stretched-grid and LAM models to ensure the
ongoing performance of these key use-cases. Visibility to the evolution of the
benchmark tests is provided through a dashboard on the Anemoi catalogue. Future
work will cover benchmarking of inference.
Accuracy tests are the latest evolution of Anemoi’s test pyramid. Here a small but
representative training problem is captured, and the tests only pass if the training
achieves an equivalent accuracy relative to a tolerance. Currently only a single global
training example is used, but as with the above, there are opportunities for expansion
to cover a wider range of Anemoi-based tasks.
System-level tests allow the creation of end-to-end tests capturing specific
organisations’ workflows, from dataset creation to inference, in a workflow that is easy
to expand. A reusable workflow for these tests has been created, enabling organisations
to contribute their own use-cases without needing to create the testing infrastructure.
Currently ECMWF and MET Norway have contributed system-level tests representative
of their use-cases.
An important next step is to strengthen compatibility and regression testing so that
upgrades cannot silently change or invalidate models and workflows used by partner
organisations. This includes clearer compatibility guarantees, checkpoint migration and
validation, numerical and skill-based regression testing, and release-candidate testing
against representative end-to-end workflows. These tests will require contributions
from partner organisations, which are best placed to identify the configurations and
operational workflows for which continuity must be assured.
Unit tests being cheap, are run at a high frequency, typically on every contribution to an
active pull request into Anemoi. Integration tests, with a larger computational footprint
are run before code is merged into Anemoi and are run before significant code
contributions and every night. System level tests, with a yet larger footprint, are also
run every night, currently using the latest version of each Anemoi package. 
