in giving operational users confidence that they can adopt new releases without
unintended changes to their models or workflows.
3.3. Expanding use-cases including use of observations
A key theme of Anemoi’s evolution in the last year has been expanding use-cases, in line
with ATS’s scope for Anemoi. Initially Anemoi enabled building forecasting models,
covering global, stretched-grid and limited area applications. However, the same
tooling can be used with relatively small changes to support other important
operational use-cases. Two examples of this are temporal downscaling, driven by MET
Norway and ECMWF, which is now merged into Anemoi, and spatial downscaling,
which is a current activity to be concluded before the end of 2026.
Anemoi now supports bringing different datasets together as either input or output, for
example enabling an additional decoder to fine-tune a forecasting model to output
more variables or higher resolution observation data. This also includes the number of
time-slices that models can ingest and output, a development spearheaded by RMI.
Anemoi now features a range of flavours of Continuous Ranked Probability Score
(CRPS) optimisation variations, e.g. minimising the CRPS in a spectral space or across 
10 of 12 ECMWF/TAC/58(26)11
multiple length scales, and different diffusion variants. This enables the community to
explore together the optimal approaches for high resolution probabilistic modelling.
A current major development is the use of observations as additional data as inputs to
the model and predicted outputs from models, in addition to training on NWP output.
This poses an interesting technical challenge, as the data can be highly unstructured,
which requires changes to dataset handling, model design and training, and inference.
The process of fully incorporating observations in Anemoi is midway, with dataset
building work completed, model building and training at a prototype stage and
inference at a design stage. Once completed this will enhance again the flexibility,
enabling organisations to train Direct Observation Prediction (DOP) models, or hybrids
training on both analysis and observation data. This work is projected to be complete
before the end of 2026.
3.4. Compatibility across an evolving Anemoi
A recurring topic in the Anemoi community and ATS has been balancing the pace of
innovation and consistency of functionality. One example of this is the ability to
continue training a model checkpoint that was first trained with one version of Anemoi,
with a newer version of Anemoi. In the last year, spearheaded by Meteo France,
functionality to migrate model checkpoints has been introduced. This includes new
tests in the testing framework to detect issues with compatibility. For changes that
would break compatibility, contributors must provide migrations to ensure this
behaviour. Exceptions have been infrequently made by ATS when maintaining
compatibility would ensure significant technical overhead.
The next step for this compatibility work, which has just begun, is compatibility of the
configuration files used to orchestrate Anemoi, which will follow a similar recipe to
checkpoint migrations. The combination of checkpoint and configuration migrations
will provide significant improvements for Anemoi users and minimise GPU hours spent
on retraining models from scratch.
4. Anemoi user/developer survey
At the most recent in-person Machine Learning Project (MLP) event, held in
Switzerland, the first survey of the Anemoi developer and contributor community was
conducted. Over 50 responses were gathered from 18 organisations. The questions
covered their use of Anemoi, confidence in contributing to it, and views on the existing
governance structure. Half the respondents had not yet reviewed code and did not feel
part of the developer group, despite the majority of the group using Anemoi on a
weekly or more frequent basis. Widening the developer group and building user
confidence in making code contributions were identified as priorities. Broadly, ATS was
viewed positively. Better prioritisation and expanded documentation, particularly for
improved onboarding, were the most common requests. When surveyed on activities to
deemphasise, there were no consistent messages, suggesting that most Anemoi
developments are viewed as useful additions.
ECMWF/TAC/58(26)11 11 of 12
Revisiting this survey regularly, including its use beyond the MLP, will provide a useful
way to track community views, needs and concerns as Anemoi evolves, and to feed
them into Anemoi governance meetings. Future surveys should include the wider
international user and contributor community, while distinguishing between open
community input and the responsibilities of organisations contributing to Anemoi’s
European public-service and operational objectives.
5. Outlook
In the past year, Anemoi has taken significant steps towards maturity, with two key
themes being testing and ecosystem expansion to enable the next-generation
operational systems. Anemoi’s use, both in Europe and beyond, continues to increase,
including NOAA’s use of Anemoi to develop its operational data-driven modelling
capabilities, a mark of Anemoi’s success. This wider uptake illustrates the value of
combining strong European public-service anchoring with open international
participation and contribution.
One topic for the coming year is deciding when to declare Anemoi version 1. Version 1
should be linked to explicit readiness criteria rather than being a primarily symbolic
milestone. At a minimum, these criteria should cover release and compatibility policy,
checkpoint migration and validation, regression and end-to-end testing, and the
associated user and developer documentation. The complete criteria and the process
for assessing them should be agreed through the evolving governance arrangements.
Another important topic will be the effective and efficient incorporation of the
developments from the wider ML community, including in the framework of EU funded
projects such as Destination Earth, WeatherGenerator or EarthGenerator.
Effective use of coding agents promises to continue as a topic over the following years.
ATS currently encourages exploration of the use of coding agents to establish where
such tools are most and least valuable. Large-scale adoption of coding agents as the
primary development tool was considered premature, particularly considering the
current state of development of testing infrastructure. Use of coding agents as tools to
empower developers was endorsed, including agents to conduct first reviews of code
contributions. In the development of Anemoi nexus, which acts as a catalogue for
Anemoi datasets used across the MLP, an exploratory model of prompt requirementdriven development has been adopted. This choice was made as the database and web
aspects involved have clear examples in the wider world where LLMs are a powerful
tool. With the current rapid pace of improvement of LLMs as coding agents, having an
agile approach to their use will help Anemoi thrive.
More broadly, the community will need mechanisms that bring together developments
currently taking place through different projects and organisational settings. The
emerging six-monthly review meeting is a first step; the value of a wider periodic
community forum, open to operational services, research organisations and other
contributors, could also be considered as the ecosystem develops.
Key to Anemoi’s continued success will be the balance between incorporating and
enabling scientific advances without compromising operational reliability. The current 
