REFINED VERSION
==================================================================

1. Flexibility and resilience go together

The paper frames the challenge as balancing rapid adoption against operational resilience. I would argue these goals reinforce each other: flexible frameworks are built on stable, well-defined interfaces, which give operational users their guarantees and researchers a clear place to plug in new ideas. This corresponds to the "maintainable architecture" the paper lists under sustainability (Section 1), and it is currently the weakest part of Anemoi.

2. Anemoi needs explicit interfaces between its components

AnemoiTrainer combines a task, a training method and a model, but the contracts between them are implicit: base classes share code rather than define behaviour, training methods reach into model internals, and the model interface assumes the existing encoder–processor–decoder design. As a result, each new feature touches several components at once — Section 3.3 notes that observations require changes to "dataset handling, model design and training, and inference". Effort currently goes mainly into adding features on this structure rather than into the interfaces that would make features cheap to add. Stable, architecture-agnostic interfaces would let new models and training methods be developed in isolation, make it easier to adopt advances from the wider ML community, and best serve the research users the scope statement aims for ("Who is Anemoi for?", Section 2): the core effort maintains the extension points, and contributing organisations implement what they need behind them.

3. The real tension is performance versus flexibility

The performance work in Section 3.1 is impressive, but I think the genuine trade-off is between performance and flexibility. Optimisations such as end-to-end sharding and custom Triton kernels are often tied to a specific architecture or data layout. Clear interfaces would help here too, by making explicit which optimisations are general and which are model-specific.

Suggestion: since the paper calls for explicit version 1 readiness criteria (Section 5), documented, stable interfaces between trainer, task, training method and model should be among them.

