- "they" vs "we": DMI is listed as an ATS member (Section 2), and the paper stresses co-development. Writing "this is what they need to do" positions you as an outsider and can read as blaming ECMWF. The refined version uses neutral phrasing ("core development effort", "contributing organisations"). Consider "we" if you want to signal shared responsibility.

- Graph-model deprecation: the paper does not mention this. If it comes from ATS discussions, note that the paper says ATS minutes are shared only with ATS and MLP attendees, so citing it to a wider audience may be inappropriate — and readers can't verify it from the document. I replaced it with a general point about maintenance. If you keep it, cite where it was discussed.

- "ECMWF's primary role should be exposing interfaces ... rather than implementing the features for them": this is the most likely sentence to cause friction. The paper credits ECMWF with most of the infrastructure (e.g. testing, Section 3.2) and asks other organisations to contribute more. Framed as "the core role", it is a fair architectural argument; framed as "ECMWF shouldn't build features", it can sound dismissive of that work. The refined version softens it accordingly.

- "brute forcing new features": loaded wording; replaced with a description of the effect, supported by the paper's own observations example (3.3).

- "very very very good job", "TRUE conflict": too informal for a committee-paper comment; emphasis works better through the argument.

- "adopting to new advances" → "adapting to" / "adopting".

- The unfinished "So in terms of 3.3." — I folded 3.3 into point 2 as evidence. If you had a different point in mind for 3.3 (e.g. downscaling or multi-dataset support), it would need its own paragraph.

- Points 2 and 3 are the strongest but are only asserted. One concrete, checkable example (e.g. the autoencoder being a config combination of task, model and graph with nothing validating it, or the abstract task base class defining no abstract methods) would make the argument much harder to dismiss.

- Point 6 is new: the original makes a critique but no request. Since the paper invites comments on v1 readiness criteria, tying the critique to a concrete, actionable ask makes it more likely to be acted on.

